<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herisau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herisau is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the capital of the half-canton of Appenzell Ausserrhoden. A market centre of the linen and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade, it grew into the canton's largest town. It shared the business of government with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trogen">Trogen</a> after Ausserrhoden's separation in 1597 and is now the seat of the cantonal government and parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herisau.jpg" alt="Herisau" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Zumbühl · GFDL · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herisau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Herisau lies in the former District of Hinterland, at the crossing point of two major routes through the region, the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a> to the Toggenburg and the road from Gossau to Appenzell. It consists of the village of Herisau and scattered hamlets, together with residential and industrial districts. About one third of the population of the whole half-canton lives there. Before 1648 the municipality controlled about twice as much land as it does now, since Schwellbrunn was then part of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Herisau was first mentioned in 837 as Herinisauva, and its church is mentioned in 907. The place suffered repeatedly in the struggles with the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>. It was destroyed in 1084 during battles around the monastery, and again in 1248 and 1249 when the monastery destroyed it to establish loyalty. In 1401 Herisau joined an alliance with other places in Appenzell during the Appenzell Wars, and between 1517 and 1518 it managed to buy itself free from the monastery.</p><p>Herisau was already an important centre of trade and commerce by 1537. Its town hall was built in 1601, and in 1606 the town was largely destroyed by fire. In 1648 Schwellbrunn separated and became an independent village. Between 1798 and 1803 Herisau was the capital of the canton of Säntis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Herisau is the capital of the canton of Appenzell Ausserrhoden and the seat of its government and parliament, while the judicial authorities are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trogen">Trogen</a>. Several buildings are listed as heritage sites of national significance: the central hamlet and the houses around the central square, the Protestant church of 1580, the houses Wetter and zur Rose, both of 1737, the hamlet of Schwänberg, and the government building with the state archive.</p><p>The majority of residents speak German. In 2003 Herisau was named Alpine Town of the Year, and with other Alpine towns it takes part in the association of that name, which works to put the Alpine Convention into effect and to achieve sustainable development in the Alpine region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herisau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Without the old capital, its Landsgemeinde met alternately in Trogen and Hundwil, and government business was shared between Trogen and <b>Herisau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/appenzell-ausserrhoden">Appenzell Ausserrhoden</a></li></ul></section>
